Integer coefficients for the element routines in this add-on.


Base functions (shape functions)
--------------------------------

See file addons/README_basefunctions


Numerical integration
---------------------

See file addons/README_Gauss


Procedure pointers
------------------

Summary of assignment of user functions/subroutines to procedure pointers:

  coefficients%set_ninti_user => set_ninti_user
  coefficients%set_Gauss_integration_user => set_Gauss_integration_user

NOTES:

 1) The user functions/subroutines only need to be assigned if they are
    actually being used. If a required assignment is forgotten an error message
    with be given.
 2) The user is free to choose the functions/subroutines for assignment as
    long as the interface is correct.


Oldvectors
----------

oldvectors%v(1) : the input vector on the old mesh
oldvectors%p(1) : the problem for the input vector on the old mesh
oldvectors%m(1) : the old mesh (for coefficients%i(5)=0).


List of the coefficients
------------------------

1   interpolation for the input vector (on old mesh)
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral, also the polynomial order p needs to
          be set with coefficients%i(6)

2   number of components (ncompv) of the input vector (on old mesh)

3   interpolation for the shape of the element (on new mesh)
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral, also the polynomial order p needs to
          be set with coefficients%i(7)

4   interpolation for the projection (on new mesh)
    NOTE: for high-order or spectral, also the polynomial order p needs to
          be set with coefficients%i(8)

5   Indicator for the meshes:
    0: new mesh is different from the old mesh. The argument mesh defines the
       new mesh and oldvectors%m(1) defines the old mesh.
    1: new mesh is identical to old mesh. The argument mesh defines the 
       new mesh and oldvectors%m(1) does not have to be defined and/or
       associated.

6   Polynomial order p of spectral/high-order interpolation for input vector
    (on old mesh)

7   Polynomial order p of spectral/high-order interpolation for the shape of 
    the element (on new mesh)

8   Polynomial order p of spectral/high-order interpolation for the projection
    (on new mesh)

9   Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

10  numerical integration rule (intrule) for the internal domain integrals
    of elements.

11  secondary numerical integration rule (intrule2) for the internal domain
    integrals of elements. This is only relevant for prisms and pyramids.

12-22 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

23  coordinate system for 2D elements:
    0: Cartesian planar
    1: Axisymmetrical
    Note that for axisymmetrical coordinates we have (z,r), i.e. the first
    coordinate is the axial coordinate z and the second coordinate is the
    radial coordinate r.

24-31 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

32  nsubint: number of subdomains for the numerical integration rule for the
    internal domain integrals of elements:
    0: default: the same as giving 1
    1: only one subdomain, which means apply the numerical integration rule
       given by coefficients%i(10) to the complete element domain.
    >1: divide the element into nsubint subdomains (in each coordinate 
        direction) and apply the numerical integration rule given by 
        coefficients%i(10) to each subdomain.

33  Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

34  Number of degrees of freedom in the element in one direction (ndfs) of the
    shape interpolation is different from the number of nodes in an element.
    0: no
    1: yes. This is tricky stuff, but it might work. The problem is
       the mapping of the elements. At the moment it is assumed that the
       first 1:ndfs nodes are the nodes of the shape unknowns, in that order.
       In this way it is possible to use elements with an additional internal
       node without a shape unknown, provided that it has the highest nodal
       number.
       A more general mechanism for mixing different shape functions/element
       types is planned but is not available yet.

35-36  Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

37  Numerical integration rule and number of subdomains for the
    volume integrals of elements:
     0: the same for all elements as given by coefficients%i(10) and
        coefficients%i(32)
     1: given by the vector per element oldvectors%e(1)
     2: given by user subroutines

38  Layer number. Needs to be specified if the elements are going to be used
    (build,derive etc) for a single layer only.
     0: default: all degrees
     >0: layer number. 

39  Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

40  Type of integration (inttype):
      0: standard Gauss-Legendre (analytical expressions)
      1: Gauss-Legendre-Lobatto
      2: mapped Gauss (quad->triangle, hexahedron->pyramid).
      3: standard Gauss-Legendre (numerical tables)
    NOTE: this affects all numerical integration of interior integrals.

41  Specifies how the integration rules must be set:
    0: default, i.e. direcly with the value in coefficients.
    1: by specifying the minimal polynomial order that the rule must be able
       to integrate exactly.
    Note, that the value of coefficients%i(41) is overruled by setting
    SET_GAUSS_BY_ORDER = .true. in the module limits_m.

42--100 Reserved for future use. Set to zero for future compatibility.

